Here's an example of how to use Natural to generate a summary of the changes made to a file:
import * as natural from 'natural';

function generateSummary(fileContent: string, changes: string[]): string {
  const tokenizer = new natural.WordTokenizer();
  const tokens = tokenizer.tokenize(fileContent);

  const addedTokens = changes
    .filter(change => change.startsWith('+'))
    .map(change => change.slice(1))
    .flatMap(tokenizer.tokenize);

  const removedTokens = changes
    .filter(change => change.startsWith('-'))
    .map(change => change.slice(1))
    .flatMap(tokenizer.tokenize);

  const summaryTokens = tokens
    .filter(token => !removedTokens.includes(token))
    .concat(addedTokens);

  const summary = summaryTokens.join(' ');

  return summary;
}
This function uses the WordTokenizer from the Natural library to tokenize the file content into words. It then uses the filter method to get the added and removed tokens from the changes, and the concat method to combine them with the remaining tokens from the file. Finally, it joins the summary tokens into a string and returns it.

